{"parentUuid":"6b59396d-cabf-420e-b862-49ec34393128","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aef4c071a06991685","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01B5Xy44fyFCGN23rB9VHVZg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011P93xk3JFeKJizLJfF8FzL","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://www.hexaly.com/templates/capacitated-facility-location-problem-cflp","prompt":"Extract any information about how Hexaly solves the problem internally: multi-start, local search, neighborhood moves, no proof of optimality, anytime behavior."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5309,"cache_read_input_tokens":23079,"output_tokens":469,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5309},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":469,"cache_read_input_tokens":23079,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5309,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5309,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011Caoxosk6bD4yayYgZeDSw","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"0158192b-49a2-446e-a15c-0df4ab8b7369","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:21.729Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"642fe737-6b79-4be2-be3a-e8dd9d1062ce","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"aef4c071a06991685","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XoMhfNdqrqnS3sgpyfgf3r","type":"tool_result","content":"# Hexaly vs. Gurobi: Benchmark Comparison\n\nBased on the webpage content provided, **specific numerical benchmark data is not included**. The page references benchmark comparisons but does not present concrete metrics like time-to-incumbent, gap-to-best, or percentage improvements.\n\n## Available Information:\n\n**Problem Types Compared:**\nThe page lists 13 benchmark categories where Hexaly and Gurobi are evaluated:\n- Traveling Salesman Problem (TSP)\n- Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem (CVRP)\n- Flexible Job Shop Scheduling (FJSP)\n- Bin Packing Problem (BPP)\n- Resource-Constrained Project Scheduling (RCPSP)\n- Quadratic Assignment Problem (QAP)\n- K-Means Clustering\n- Maintenance Scheduling\n- Car Sequencing with Paint-Shop Batching\n- Assembly Line Balancing (SALBP)\n- Job Shop Scheduling (JSSP)\n- Vehicle Routing with Time Windows (CVRPTW)\n\n## Key Claim:\n\nThe page states: \"Does Hexaly outperform Gurobi on all types of optimization problems?\" with the response that they \"don't claim Hexaly outperforms Gurobi on all problems.\"\n\n**To access actual benchmark metrics**, you would need to visit the individual benchmark pages linked from this comparison page."}]},"uuid":"8c316f8f-ff2d-4d54-b0e2-2a3781e47d75","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:25.151Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"642fe737-6b79-4be2-be3a-e8dd9d1062ce","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"0158192b-49a2-446e-a15c-0df4ab8b7369","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"aef4c071a06991685","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011P93xk3JFeKJizLJfF8FzL","type":"tool_result","content":"# Hexaly's Internal Problem-Solving Approach\n\nBased on the provided content, **no specific information is disclosed** about Hexaly's internal solving mechanisms for the Capacitated Facility Location Problem.\n\nThe documentation describes *what* the solver does—it minimizes total facility and allocation costs—but does not explain *how* it works internally. There is no mention of:\n\n- Multi-start strategies\n- Local search techniques\n- Neighborhood move operators\n- Optimality guarantees or lack thereof\n- Anytime algorithm behavior\n\nThe page only notes that users can \"parameterize the solver\" (e.g., setting time limits) and that Hexaly is described as a \"Hybrid Optimization Solver,\" but the actual algorithmic approach remains undisclosed in this documentation.\n\nTo learn about Hexaly's internal mechanisms, you would likely need to consult the detailed API documentation, research papers, or contact Hexaly directly."}]},"uuid":"228c3c40-9122-44de-aa35-e45a122087e4","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:25.600Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0158192b-49a2-446e-a15c-0df4ab8b7369","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6b59396d-cabf-420e-b862-49ec34393128","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"aef4c071a06991685","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DzoUTcD7MfrpvrB81f4Cty","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Hexaly warm start initial solution callback heuristic limitation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Setting an Initial Solution — Hexaly 14.5 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://www.hexaly.com/docs/last/technicalfeatures/initialsolution.html\"},{\"title\":\"Warm start in callback to improve solution – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/360057910052-Warm-start-in-callback-to-improve-solution\"},{\"title\":\"How to use a solution from a heuristic algorithm as the initial solution of Gurobi – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/360059687012-How-to-use-a-solution-from-a-heuristic-algorithm-as-the-initial-solution-of-Gurobi\"},{\"title\":\"Using an initial solution while also using lazy constraints – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/10954065407505-Using-an-initial-solution-while-also-using-lazy-constraints\"},{\"title\":\"Re: Warm Start / Using a heuristic feasible solution\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/ampl/c/yI6HcMIn0ts\"},{\"title\":\"www.hexaly.com www.hexaly.com Hexaly Optimizer for Scheduling\",\"url\":\"https://schedulingseminar.com/presentations/SchedulingSeminar_PhillipeLaborie.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Setting an initial solution — Hexaly 14.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://www.hexaly.com/docs/last/technicalfeatures/initialsolution.html?redirect\"},{\"title\":\"Re: Warm Start / Using a heuristic feasible solution - Google Groups\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/forum/?_escaped_fragment_=topic%2Fampl%2FyI6HcMIn0ts\"},{\"title\":\"Hexaly Optimizer Class — Hexaly 14.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://www.hexaly.com/docs/last/modelerreference/standardlibrary/hexalymodule/hexalyoptimizer.html\"},{\"title\":\"Injecting Heuristic Solution with Callback is Very Slow – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/360077367271-Injecting-Heuristic-Solution-with-Callback-is-Very-Slow\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about Hexaly and warm start/initial solution functionality, though the specific combination of \"callback\" and \"limitation\" in relation to heuristics wasn't extensively covered in the results.\n\nHere's what I found:\n\nHexaly Optimizer does not need a starting solution to launch its algorithms, however in some cases you may want to force Hexaly Optimizer to start from a specific solution. Hexaly supports initial solutions (warm start), indicating that the optimizer has this feature built in.\n\nRegarding callbacks in Hexaly, when a callback is called, the solver is paused, and you can stop the resolution from a callback, retrieve the current solution, retrieve the statistics of the solver and call all the methods marked as \"allowed in state PAUSED\".\n\nHowever, the search results don't contain specific information about limitations when combining warm start initial solutions with callback-based heuristics in Hexaly. The results mainly show discussions about similar challenges in other solvers like Gurobi, where there can be complications with using heuristic solutions in callbacks.\n\nIf you need more detailed information about Hexaly-specific limitations regarding warm start and callbacks, you may want to consult the official Hexaly documentation directly or reach out to their support team.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"9146b0b3-b922-455b-a24a-82493f377a11","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:26.493Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6b59396d-cabf-420e-b862-49ec34393128","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"86c53f74-0989-45c0-9117-f5d8fbc0ad26","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"aef4c071a06991685","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DMXqxDUfwrwPRbnoJ8Mc8E","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Hexaly 2D bin packing rectangle placement strip packing Python model\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - secnot/rectpack: Python 2D rectangle packing library · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/secnot/rectpack\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - SebastianBitsch/bin-packing: Python solver for the 2D bin packing problem · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/SebastianBitsch/bin-packing\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - AlkiviadisAleiferis/hyperpack: A 2D bin/strip-packing problem solving python package\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/AlkiviadisAleiferis/hyperpack\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - ktnr/BinPacking2D: Exact solutions for two-dimensional bin packing problems by branch-and-cut · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ktnr/BinPacking2D\"},{\"title\":\"Bin Packing Problem (BPP)\",\"url\":\"https://www.hexaly.com/templates/bin-packing-problem-bpp\"},{\"title\":\"Bin Packing (BPP) — Hexaly 13.5 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://www.hexaly.com/docs/last/exampletour/binpacking.html\"},{\"title\":\"hyperpack · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/hyperpack/\"},{\"title\":\"Online calculator: 2D Bin Packing Problem Solver\",\"url\":\"https://planetcalc.com/8634/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - juj/RectangleBinPack: Source code for performing 2d rectangular bin packing. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/juj/RectangleBinPack\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - seanys/2D-Irregular-Packing-Algorithm: Realize 2D irregular packing algorithm with python · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/seanys/2D-Irregular-Packing-Algorithm\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found about Hexaly and 2D bin packing/strip packing with Python models:\n\n## Hexaly Bin Packing\n\nIn the Bin Packing Problem (BPP), items with known weights must be assigned to bins with uniform capacity. Each item must be placed inside exactly one bin, and the total weight of items inside each bin must not exceed its capacity. The objective is to minimize the number of bins used.\n\nThe Hexaly model for the Bin Packing Problem uses set decision variables. For each bin, a set variable represents the set of items assigned to that bin. The set variables are constrained to form a partition, ensuring that each item belongs to exactly one bin.\n\nThe Python model uses `hexaly.optimizer` and includes code to read instance data, define set decision variables for bins, and create partition constraints.\n\nHexaly Optimizer achieves an average gap of 0.2% on the Bin Packing Problem in 1 minute of running time on instances from the BPPLIB research benchmark, with up to 5,500 items to pack.\n\n## Alternative Python Libraries for 2D Packing\n\nWhile the search results don't show a specific Hexaly 2D rectangle placement model, I found other Python libraries for rectangle packing:\n\nThe hyperpack library is an API for solving instances of the 2D Binpacking problem and strip packing instances. The library is multiprocessing enabled to minimize execution times and utilizes only pure Python, making the package dependency free.\n\nRectpack is a collection of heuristic algorithms for solving the 2D knapsack problem, also known as the bin packing problem. In essence, it involves packing a set of rectangles into the smallest number of bins.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"4a29bf3c-f105-48d1-91ce-b0d4876f288c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:27.293Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"86c53f74-0989-45c0-9117-f5d8fbc0ad26","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
